<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape of Good Hope</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cape of Good Hope is a headland on the southwestern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, rounded by Portuguese navigators in the late fifteenth century. The sea route around it allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> to trade directly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>'s monopoly covered all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trade east of the Cape. The route diverted trade away from the Middle Eastern routes controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope.jpg" alt="Cape of Good Hope" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Playa_Dias,_Cape_Point,_Sud%C3%A1frica,_2018-07-23,_DD_103.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
